  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n### What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx (the voice box). It helps protect the walls and the frontal part of the larynx, including the vocal cords, which are located directly behind it.\n\n### Do Women Have One Too?\n\nAdam's apples are found on both women and men \u2014 they just show up more prominently in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n### Why Are Men's Larger?\n\nThe main reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n1. **Hormonal differences:** In men, the production of testosterone causes the larynx to grow larger, resulting in a deeper voice and a more prominent Adam's apple.\n\n2. **Size of the voice box:** During puberty, a man's voice box tends to grow in size much more than in women, so a noticeable bump appears right in the middle of the throat.\n\n3. **Structural differences:** There is also a difference in the *shape* of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n4. **Position of the cartilage:** In females, the laryngeal protuberance sits on the upper edge of the thyroid cartilage, and the larynx tends to be smaller in size, so the \"bump\" caused by the protrusion is much less visible or not discernible.\n\n### Starting Point Is the Same\n\nThe Adam's apple is about the same size in people assigned male at birth and those assigned female at birth until puberty. Once puberty starts, hormones \u2014 especially testosterone \u2014 cause the vocal cords to grow bigger in males.\n\n### Why Does Voice Pitch Matter?\n\nSome people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box. People with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one. This is why men, on average, have deeper voices than women.\n\n### Can Women Have a More Prominent Adam's Apple?\n\nYes! Women who have higher levels of testosterone, either naturally or due to medical conditions, may experience more masculine traits, including a larger larynx and a more noticeable Adam's apple. For example, conditions such as polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can cause an increase in testosterone levels in women, potentially contributing to a more prominent laryngeal prominence.\n\nIn summary, the larger Adam's apple in men is primarily driven by the surge of testosterone during puberty, which causes the larynx and surrounding cartilage to grow significantly larger than in women.",
